  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nQuiet weather conditions will continue through the day Friday, with \na trend toward a more active weekend in terms of shower and storm \nchances. \n\nProgression of upper level pattern will allow an upstream upper \nlevel ridge to build across the Great Lakes region into Friday \nmorning. Corresponding low level anticyclone will be centered across \nthe region tonight providing light and variable winds. Some high \nclouds may spill over the dampening ridge across the northern \nPlains, but are not expected to have a significant impact on temps \ntonight (seasonable low temps from upper 50s to lower 60s) with\ndecent radiational cooling conditions.\n\nAttention for later Friday into the weekend will be on evolution of \nan upper level short evident across Idaho/western Montana this \nafternoon. This short wave is progged to drop southeast down the \nridge through the day Friday and into the Lower Ohio River Valley by \nSunday morning. Convection is expected to develop tonight across the \nDakotas as a low level jet preceding this disturbance enhances low \nlevel moisture convergence. Convection associated with this feature \nwill act to modify this short wave from tonight through Friday night \nwhich leads to some uncertainty in strength and track of associated \nlow/mid level features. Some dProg/dT trends have indicated a slight \nslowing trend with the mid level trough evolution which may keep \nhigher rain chances into Sunday east of Interstate 69. Still a good \ndeal of uncertainty remains with track of this system given upper \nlevel synoptic tendencies favoring a more suppressed track of low \nlevel reflection into southern IL/southern IN, while \ndiabatically generated low/mid level PV anomalies will tend to \nwant to steer track across the southern Great Lakes. It remains \nunclear to what extent this northward displaced diabatic PV \nanomaly influences track. Nonetheless, will continue idea of \nprevious forecast with rain chances increasing sharply from \nwest to east Friday night into Saturday morning as advective \nforcing increases downstream of the wave. May need to watch for \npotential of some locally heavier rainfall amounts Friday night \ninto Saturday particularly if deformation forcing can anchor for\na time across portions of the area. PWATs of 1.75+ inches are \npossible, and potential does exist for warm cloud depths in \nexcess of 12k feet. If the more amplified/northward solution \npans out, cannot completely discount some isolated wind \npotential during the afternoon/early evening Saturday given this\nsolution would at least be moderately sheared in weakly \nunstable environment. Early indications would suggest if \nisolated severe threat does evolve, it would be most likely \nsouth of US-24 across northeast IN/northwest OH which may be \nlocated at nose of instability axis. \n\nFor Sunday and Monday, pattern features a bit of a rex-block look \nwith the possibility that upper low becomes stagnant across the \nLower OH Valley as longwave ridging pushes across eastern Ontario \nand western Quebec. This blocking will likely slow the warming trend \nand delay arrival of next significant low level thermal ridge, or \nkeep brunt of this west-east thermal ridging back across the \nPlains/Corn Belt. An active southern Canada pattern will likely \ndampen upstream longwave ridging enough for periodic chances of \nshowers and storms Wednesday through Friday along with near\nseasonable temps.",
